From 089e0a4b232aa29ff4935063f5f12256f0947386 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 15:39:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] x86 debug registers: DR0-DR3, DR6 and DR7 per thread,
 instruction and data breakpoints

FEX kept no debug register state: SetThreadContext with CONTEXT_DEBUG_REGISTERS did nothing and no hardware
breakpoint ever fired, which debuggers, crash reporters and programs watching their own memory rely on. Measured
against x86-64 Windows Server 2022 and 2025 with tests/x64-fidelity/drtest (64-bit and 32-bit).

State and compilation: CPUState holds dr[4], dr6 and dr7 (DR7 as Windows keeps it) and resume_flag_rip. SetDebugRegisters
turns them into the thread's breakpoints (X86State::DebugBreakpoints: instruction breakpoints by address, data
breakpoints as aligned ranges; only the local enables count, as on Windows, and an instruction breakpoint with LEN other
than 0 never fires there). They are compiled into the thread's code: its code cache is cleared when they change, and a
thread with breakpoints moves to a code buffer of its own, so no other thread runs its blocks or it theirs, and the disk
cache is not used for it.

Instruction breakpoints: a block that reaches a breakpoint address ends before it, so the check (DebugBreakpoint) runs
at a block start, where the guest state is all in CPUState: B bits in DR6, then #DB (EXCEPTION_SINGLE_STEP) with RIP at
the instruction. A context loaded with RF set lets that instruction run once (resume_flag_rip, so EFLAGS never keeps a
stale RF), and the context of a fault reports RF set as Windows does (ud2, divide errors, access violations, DEP, hlt,
not int3 or a single step), so continuing from a fault does not take the instruction's breakpoint again.

Data breakpoints: every guest memory access (loads, stores, pairs, vector element and masked forms, x87 stores, push
and pop, atomics) checks the thread's ranges and sets the B bit and CpuStateFrame::DebugWatchHit. A thread with data
breakpoints runs one instruction per block, so the check at the next block start raises the trap right after the
instruction, with exact state; REP string instructions then run one element per execution (MOVS, STOS, LODS, SCAS,
CMPS), so a hit traps with RIP at the instruction and RCX the count left, as on x86. The checks leave NZCV alone, which
holds the guest's flags between instructions.

Cost: none for a thread without breakpoints (the doorbell check at every block start now loads 64 bits, which also
cover the two debug event bytes); a thread with data breakpoints runs about three times slower while they are set.

Windows: the registers cross the ntdll boundary in contexts, like the rest of the x64 state. The ARM64EC module takes
them from a context it is entered with (CONTEXT_DEBUG_REGISTERS at BeginSimulation, so in a context ntdll continues a
thread with) and puts them in every context it builds (exceptions, suspension, system calls), in the ARM64 breakpoint
registers (Bvr[0-5], tag "64DR" in Bcr[7]), as Windows reports them: all 0 unless DR7 keeps them (L0-L3, LE, GE), DR7
with bit 10 set, DR6 with the bits x86 reads as 1 and the B bits of a hit being reported. A suspension context keeps RF
for an instruction the thread resumed at with RF and has not run yet. The one export, KeepX64DebugRegisters, says what
x86-64 Windows keeps of the registers a context sets (nothing while DR7 enables none, DR7 through the legal mask, never
DR6; they read back so until the thread runs with them), and its presence tells ntdll that the emulator implements
them. ntdll keeps a thread's registers while it runs native code or waits and hands changes back with the next context
the emulator is entered with, ringing the doorbell when the thread goes back to x64 code through native code
(highball-engine patches-arm64/0029). Recompiling from a context load runs with ntdll's memory callbacks off. The WoW64
module takes them from the 32-bit context, where Wine keeps them: a reload can happen inside a block (after a system
call), so the thread applies them at its next block start; they read back as WoW64 reports them (LE added for an
address with nothing enabled, DR7 through 0xffff0155).

On an M4: drtest and drtest32 71 of 71, and drdbg and drdbg32 25 of 25 (another process sets and reads them at debug
events, on threads it suspended in x64 code or in a wait, and on a process created suspended), as on x86-64 Windows;
ctxtest 546 of 546.
